<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flinders Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flinders Island is the largest island of the Furneaux Group in Bass Strait, north-east of Tasmania. From 1833 the surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a>, gathered by George Augustus Robinson, were confined at the settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wybalenna">Wybalenna</a> on its west coast, where many died of disease. The remaining people were moved to Oyster Cove near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a> in 1847.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flinders-island.jpg" alt="Flinders Island" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flinders_island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Flinders Island covers about 1,367 square kilometres in the Bass Strait, northeast of the island of Tasmania, of which it is now part, and lies 54 kilometres from Cape Portland on 40° south, in the zone known as the Roaring Forties. It is about 62 kilometres from north to south and 37 kilometres from east to west. Granite ridges run the length of the island, and about a third of it is mountainous and rugged. Mount Strzelecki in the south west, at 782 metres, is its highest peak. Sandy dunes and coastal lagoons mark the eastern shore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People first reached the island at least 35,000 years ago, when they crossed the land bridge that later became Bass Strait. A population remained until about 4,500 years ago and then died out, succumbing to thirst and hunger after an acute <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a> climate shift. Tobias Furneaux recorded some of the south-eastern islands of the group in 1773, and Matthew Flinders charted the islands in 1798 before completing, with George Bass, the first circumnavigation of Tasmania, which proved it to be an island. Flinders called the largest island Great Island. Phillip Parker King later named it after Flinders.</p><p>In the late 18th century sealers and Aboriginal women, most of them kidnapped from mainland groups, often lived on the island. Seal stocks collapsed, and the last sealing permit was issued in 1828. Many sealers' families stayed, living by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> grazing and muttonbirding. From 1831 the remnants of the Tasmanian Aboriginal population were exiled first to a place called The Lagoons, and in 1833 to the Wybalenna Aboriginal Establishment at Settlement Point. About 180 survivors were held there, and around 130 died at Wybalenna alone.</p><p>After a campaign against the commandant, Henry Jeanneret, that included a petition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, the remaining 47 people were moved in 1847 to Oyster Cove Station, 56 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a>. Freehold land was given out from the late 19th century, the Municipality of Flinders Island was set up in 1903, and a proper settlement scheme began only in the 1950s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island is part of the Municipality of Flinders Island and is the only island of the Furneaux Group with more than one permanent settlement. Whitemark, which has the main airstrip, and Lady Barron are the largest settlements. Strzelecki National Park covers the area around Mount Strzelecki, and two stretches of the island are recognised as Important Bird Areas. The island is the only remaining habitat of a subspecies of the common wombat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Flinders Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Black War of the 1820s colonists and soldiers killed many Aboriginal Tasmanians, and the survivors were removed to Wybalenna on <b>Flinders Island</b> in the 1830s.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-tasmania">Colony of Tasmania</a></li></ul></section>
